---
import BaseLayout from "src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title="TWONy-macro">
  <div class="min-h-full w-full bg-slate-800 text-slate-300">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-16 md:py-36">
      <h1 class="mb-2 text-3xl font-bold md:text-6xl">Introduction</h1>

      <p class="mb-4 leading-6">
        Welcome to TWONy-macro, an interactive demonstration that shows how
        network structure and algorithmic choices shape opinion formation in
        online communities. Watch as virtual agents with evolving sentiment
        states interact in a 3D network visualization, creating patterns of
        opinion convergence, clustering, and polarization. This simulation helps
        visualize how the topology of social networks and recommendation
        algorithms influence the collective emotional dynamics of digital
        communities.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-2 text-sm leading-5">
        Explore different neighbor selection strategies - from random
        interactions to similarity-based or sentiment-biased connections - and
        observe how these algorithmic choices affect whether communities
        converge toward consensus or fragment into opposing camps. Everything
        runs locally in your browser: no data is transmitted, tracked, or stored
        on a server.
      </p>

      <div class="mx-auto my-8 text-center">
        <a
          class="inline-block rounded-lg bg-slate-300 px-4 py-2 font-bold text-slate-800 hover:bg-white"
          href={`${base}/simulation`}
        >
          &gt; to simulation
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-xl md:text-3xl">How It Works</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 leading-6">
        Every agent holds a sentiment between 0 (negative, red) and 1 (positive,
        green) and follows a fixed number of other agents. In each step, a
        sorting algorithm picks one of the followed agents as the interaction
        partner. The simulation implements the Deffuant-Weisbuch Bounded
        Confidence Model: the agent only moves toward the partner's sentiment if
        both are within a certain "confidence threshold" - otherwise the
        encounter is ignored.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-xl md:text-3xl">Sorting Algorithms</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 leading-6">
        The sorting strategy stands in for a platform's recommendation algorithm
        - it decides whose content an agent sees:
      </p>
      <dl
        class="mb-4 grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-sm leading-5 md:grid-cols-2"
      >
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold">Random</dt>
          <dd>
            A followed agent is picked by chance - the unbiased baseline, like a
            feed without ranking.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold">Similarity</dt>
          <dd>
            The followed agent with the closest sentiment is shown. Agents
            mostly meet like-minded peers.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold">Positivity</dt>
          <dd>
            The most positive followed agent is shown, pulling sentiment upward
            wherever the confidence threshold allows.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold">Negativity</dt>
          <dd>
            The most negative followed agent is shown, pulling sentiment
            downward wherever the confidence threshold allows.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <h2 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-xl md:text-3xl">Configuration</h2>
      <ul class="mb-4 list-decimal pl-8">
        <li class="mb-2">
          <span class="font-bold">Network Configuration</span>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6">
            <li>Number of Agents: Size of the social network (5-500 nodes)</li>
            <li>
              Number of Neighbors: How many other agents each agent follows (1-5
              connections)
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li class="mb-2">
          <span class="font-bold">Opinion Dynamics (Deffuant-Weisbuch BCM)</span
          >
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6">
            <li>
              Epsilon BCM: Confidence threshold for opinion influence (0.01-1.0)
            </li>
            <li>Delta BCM: Influence strength parameter (0.01-1.0)</li>
            <li>
              Sorting Strategy: Algorithm for neighbor selection during
              interactions
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li class="mb-2">
          <span class="font-bold">Simulation Control</span>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6">
            <li>
              Number of Steps: Total simulation duration (50-1000 iterations)
            </li>
            <li>
              Real-time Controls: Start, pause, single-step, and reset, with
              adjustable speed and an optional auto loop
            </li>
            <li>
              Model parameters can be changed while the simulation runs;
              changing the network generates a new one
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-xl md:text-3xl">Reading the Results</h2>
      <ul class="mb-4 list-disc pl-8">
        <li>
          The chart traces every agent's sentiment as a thin line and the
          network average as a bold blue line.
        </li>
        <li>
          Spread (σ) is the standard deviation of all sentiments: it shrinks
          toward 0 as the network approaches consensus.
        </li>
        <li>
          Opinion clusters counts the groups of agents that remain separated on
          the sentiment scale - more than one means the network has fragmented.
        </li>
        <li>
          Hover a node in the 3D view to read an agent's current sentiment; drag
          to rotate, scroll to zoom.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mb-2 text-sm leading-5">
        This educational tool demonstrates core concepts from computational
        social science, helping users understand how algorithmic design choices
        in social media platforms can inadvertently shape the emotional
        landscape of online discourse.
      </p>

      <div class="mx-auto my-8 text-center">
        <a
          class="inline-block rounded-lg bg-slate-300 px-4 py-2 font-bold text-slate-800 hover:bg-white"
          href={`${base}/simulation`}
        >
          &gt; to simulation
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
